WHERE THE ANALYSIS WENT, AND WHY IT IS NOT KEYED ON STATIONS

This file says WHAT IT DOES. It used to also say what that MEANS, in
chronological prose at the foot, and Tony's 2026-08-23 offline report named
the cost: reading a station required scrolling between the analysis and the
station code, and there was nowhere for him to answer.

The meaning now lives in incant/designDocs as ProblemRecords, ONE ENTRY PER
PROBLEM. It is deliberately NOT one entry per station. This file iterates --
its station 4 next month is a different animal from its station 4 today -- so
an archive keyed on station numbers goes stale the first time the ladder
rotates, and goes stale SILENTLY. Where a problem was met is an attribute on
the record, not its identity:

    station     which station met it, in the vintage below
    vintage     the frontier seal date the station numbers refer to

Each record carries a permanent SKELETON that survives forever -- symptom,
finding, workaround, solution, status, verdict, reviewed -- plus a sheddable
description holding the prose. Trimming an entry means deleting the
description and nothing else.

    status      the lifecycle grade only: open, guess, ruled, remedy, baked,
                workaround, and the two terminal flavours remedy and
                retired-unreproduced
    verdict     Tony's disposition -- the loud channel, where FIX THIS goes
    reviewed    Tony's ledger, append-only while live, collapsing at trim

symptom is written as what a future rematch would GREP FOR, because the
trimmed archive is a have-we-fought-this-before surface and a rematch is
recognised by symptom, never by name.

TO LOOK ANY OF IT UP: edit the lookup line at the foot of incant/lookup and
run it. lookuP(bt34) queries the decoder AND the problem records in one call
and prints whichever exist, so nobody has to know which file to ask.
tombstone(bt34) renders a record with the prose already shed, which is what a
trim will leave. lookupIndex() prints every record with its status.

